Summary
The internal audit aligns current business practices with industry standards and best practices. It provides mitigation recommendations for high‑risk vulnerabilities and outlines a strategy to improve the organization’s security posture. The audit team documents findings, provides remediation plans, and communicates with stakeholders.

Scope
The internal IT audit will assess:

User permissions

Existing controls, procedures, and system protocols

Technology currently in use

Goals
Adhere to the NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF)

Establish policies and procedures for regulatory compliance

Fortify system controls

Risk Assessment
Current Assets
Managed by the IT Department:

Employee equipment (desktops/laptops, smartphones, remote workstations, peripherals, surveillance cameras)

Internal network storing customer, vendor, and organizational data

Risk Description
There is inadequate asset management and a lack of proper controls. The organization may not be compliant with U.S. and international regulations.

Control Best Practices
The organization must dedicate resources to asset management and determine the impact of asset loss on business continuity.

Risk Score
8/10 (High) — due to lack of controls and compliance adherence.

Additional Comments
The likelihood of lost assets or regulatory fines is high because required controls and privacy standards for PII are not fully implemented.
